          "example": "<p>An answer can sound fluent while mixing accurate facts, mistakes and guesses. Check one claim at a time:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Supported:</strong> the source states it, or the conclusion follows directly from the supplied facts.</li>\n<li><strong>Contradicted:</strong> the source gives an incompatible fact.</li>\n<li><strong>Not given:</strong> the source does not establish the claim. It could be true elsewhere; you cannot use this source to confirm it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>“Not given” does not mean false. It means you should leave the detail open or find evidence before treating it as a fact.</p>\n<h4>Work worked check</h4>\n<p><strong>Source W3:</strong> The checklist&#x27;s assigned owner is Sam. Alex gathers feedback. The process test is next Tuesday. No announcement owner or date is recorded.</p>\n<p><strong>Draft answer:</strong> “Alex is the assigned checklist owner and will announce results on Friday.”</p>\n<p>“Alex is the assigned checklist owner” conflicts with the assigned owner Sam. Alex announcing results and the Friday date are not given. A repair is: “Sam is the checklist owner. Alex gathers feedback. Announcement ownership and timing are not specified.” The correction does not decide who should announce it.</p>",
