          "transfer": "<p>For your route, write the sample you would use and identify its intended reader. Explain one detail you deliberately leave out.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Fresh source and agreed scope</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>L4-E-X</code></td><td>A gardening group has ten helpers and meets on Saturday. The organiser agrees those two facts may appear in a public notice. Helpers&#x27; contact details must stay in the private organiser sheet.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>",
          "comparison": "<details><summary>Compare your decision</summary>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Everyday:</strong> “Ten helpers meet on Saturday” fits the notice&#x27;s stated scope. Its audience is the public; phone numbers stay out. The scope does not let you add an unconfirmed address or time.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Use the audience and scope explicitly given in your case. When the source permits a limited public statement, those facts can be used for that audience; when it names a particular recipient, keep that destination clear. The decision follows the supplied facts and destination.</p>\n</details>",
