          "transfer": "<p>Choose whether to <strong>revise</strong>, <strong>ask/decline with a reason</strong>, or <strong>record already addressed</strong>. Write the feedback response and name the source/version it concerns.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Fresh source, draft and feedback</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>L6-T-X</code></td><td>A file form should accept PDFs up to 2 MB. No test has run. Draft v1 says “3 MB PDF rejection test: passed.” Feedback says “separate the expected rejection from the actual execution status.”</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>",
          "comparison": "<details><summary>Compare your response</summary>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Technical:</strong> revise: “Expected result: reject a 3 MB PDF under the 2 MB rule. Execution: not run.” Reason: “The requirement supports the expectation, but no execution establishes a pass.”</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Different wording is fine when the decision and reason follow the supplied evidence. If your answer simply says “done,” add the specific change or the reason you did not make one.</p>\n</details>",
          "own": "<p>Use an invented artifact from the course. Ask one focused question, such as “Does my recovery step preserve what remains unverified?” Then write a sample response yourself or compare it with a worked response above. Label it as practice feedback; do not invent a real reviewer.</p>\n<p>Keep these pieces together:</p>\n<blockquote><p>Source and draft version: …</p>\n<p>Focused question: …</p>\n<p>Feedback: observation + evidence + suggestion + limit.</p>\n<p>My decision: revise / ask or decline / already addressed.</p>\n<p>New text or retained text, with a reason: …</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Self-check rubric:</strong> the comment identifies a specific issue; evidence supports its advice; the suggested action is usable; your decision has a reason; and the version history does not imply a review that never happened. If you edit the answer again, reassess any earlier self-check marks against the new text.</p>\n<p>Keep your practice record for yourself unless you deliberately choose an audience. Asking for feedback from a particular person, joining a group and proposing a public reusable example are different actions. A private save or a helpful comment does not automatically make the work public.</p>\n<p><strong>Take forward:</strong> <a href=\"#lesson=7\">complete the foundation practice</a> with one connected artifact. Keep what you can explain, mark what remains uncertain, and repeat the process with a fresh source.</p>"
